# Verifying changes

## "Compiles clean" is not "deployed"

This is a packaged MSIX app. A build compiles to `obj` and then copies to `bin`; if the app is
running it holds `AShareMotionStudio.exe` and **the copy fails while the compilation succeeds**:

```
error MSB3027: Could not copy ... apphost.exe ...
The file is locked by: "AShareMotionStudio (nnnnn)"
```

Filtering build output to `warning CS|error CS` hides this entirely, because it is an MSB error.
Stop the app before building, or expect to deploy again afterwards.

**Never `Rebuild` while the app might be running.** `Rebuild` cleans first. The clean succeeds,
the copy back fails on the locked exe, and the output folder is left without
`runtimeconfig.json` and `deps.json` — at which point the apphost reports that .NET is not
installed, which is false and sends everyone looking in the wrong place.

## Prove it from the artifact, not from the app starting

That the app launched says nothing about which build it is. Ask the deployed files:

```powershell
$bin = "src\AShareMotionStudio\bin\x64\Debug\net10.0-windows10.0.26100.0"
$b = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes("$bin\AShareMotionStudio.dll")
$u8  = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetString($b)
$u16 = [System.Text.Encoding]::Unicode.GetString($b)
```

**Search both encodings, and include a control string.** String literals live in metadata as
UTF-16, so an ASCII or UTF-8 search never finds them; decoding the whole file as UTF-16 from
byte zero misses every string starting on an odd offset. Both failures look exactly like the
change not having been built. A check that finds nothing must be shown able to find something —
search for one string you know is there and one you know is not, and require both answers.

For resources, dump the compiled PRI. A missing `x:Uid` key is a runtime XAML failure on one
page and the build says nothing:

```powershell
$mp = "C:\Program Files (x86)\Windows Kits\10\bin\10.0.26100.0\x64\makepri.exe"
& $mp dump /if "$bin\resources.pri" /of "$PWD\artifacts\pri.xml" /o
```

`PRI249: Invalid qualifier: EN-US | PT-BR | ZH-HANS | ZH-HANT` on every build is **expected and
cosmetic** — MakePri tries each hyphenated language folder name as a `qualifier-value` pair
before falling back. It was settled by confirming the dumped index holds fourteen distinct
`Language-*` qualifiers and fourteen candidates per key. Do not "fix" it; do notice if the
count ever stops being fourteen.

## Prove a package from inside it

The Store bundle is a zip of zips. Reading the build log is not the same as looking:

```powershell
Add-Type -AssemblyName System.IO.Compression.FileSystem
$b = [System.IO.Compression.ZipFile]::OpenRead("artifacts\<name>.msixbundle")
$b.Entries | ForEach-Object { $_.FullName }
```

Extract the inner per-architecture `.msix` and check it holds the fourteen help documents,
`resources.pri`, and Win2D's native component. That component is
`runtimes/<rid>/native/Microsoft.Graphics.Canvas.dll` — searching for "Win2D" or "Direct2D"
finds nothing and looks like a dependency that failed to travel.

## An audit script reports; it does not adjudicate

A throwaway script written to check this app's output knows less about the domain than the app
does, and it is trusted more because it is the thing doing the checking. Two examples already
from this repo:

- A key audit matching `Strings.Get("` called four keys unused. They are reached through
  ternaries — `Strings.Get(playing ? "StudioPause.Content" : "StudioPlay.Content")` — so the
  pattern never saw them. Match the literal *anywhere it can appear*.
- A regex looking for `"(Studio\w+)"` reported `StudioPage` as a missing resource. It was
  `<see cref="StudioPage"/>` in a doc comment.

Have these scripts **list what they found and leave the judgement**. Two numbers that disagree
are worth looking at; a verdict is worth nothing.

## What can and cannot be checked from here

Checkable: compiling, package contents, resource parity and key reachability, generated image
formats and alpha, help-document block counts.

Not checkable: whether the window opens, whether the preview letterboxes correctly, whether the
360-pixel parameter column survives German and Russian, whether an animation reads well, and
whether a translation reads naturally.

Claims should match. "Built, packaged, resources verified; the app has not been run" is the
honest shape, and it is what lets the reader spend their attention where it is actually needed.

## Verifying the render path specifically

A change to a renderer cannot be judged by a build at all, and two of its properties are claims
the README makes:

- **Preview and file agree.** Export a short video and compare a frame against the preview
  scrubbed to the same fraction. They should be identical, because it is the same code — so a
  difference is a real defect and not a rounding argument.
- **Export is faster than real time.** Time a 90-second export. If it takes 90 seconds,
  something is pacing on a clock and `one-render-path.mdc` has been broken.

### Inspect the picture, not the container

**A video can be upside down while every property you can read out of its container is correct.**
This happened. `ftyp`/`moov` were right, there was no `moof`, `mvhd` read 90.00 s for a 90-second
video, `stsz` held exactly the 2,700 frames that had been drawn, and the export finished in 28
seconds — every check passed, and every frame was mirrored about the horizontal axis, because
Win2D hands back pixels top-down and Media Foundation's uncompressed RGB samples are bottom-up.

So the container checks are necessary and **not sufficient**. At least once per encoder change,
decode a frame and look at it.

### Compare a decoded frame against a cover, not against your judgement

The instrument is **Save cover PNG**: same renderer, same `FrameContext`, no transform, and a
preview that matches it. The page parks on progress 1.0 after a fetch and exporting does not move
the scrub, so the cover and the video's *last* decoded frame are the same frame.

Compare them under all four orientations and take the lowest difference:

| orientation | mean absolute difference |
|---|---|
| as decoded | 26.99 |
| flipped vertically | **2.29** ← the frame was being flipped |
| flipped horizontally | 29.21 |
| rotated 180° | 12.85 |

That table is the whole diagnosis: which axis, in one number each, with a residue of ~2 explained
by H.264. A person looking at the video may say "rotated" or "mirrored" and be wrong about the
axis — the arithmetic will not be.

`tools/drive-studio.py` drives the app through fetch and export with `uiautomation`, so this is
repeatable without touching the mouse. Two details it encodes: the parameter panel is a
`ScrollViewer`, so a control below the fold has an empty `BoundingRectangle` and `Click()` throws —
use `GetInvokePattern().Invoke()`. And UI Automation finds the window **by title**, so it only
sees an instance running in Chinese.

## Screenshotting this app, which has three separate traps

Layout has to be looked at, so capturing the window is part of checking a UI change. Each of
these produced a confidently wrong picture before being understood.

**`PrintWindow` cannot capture the preview.** `PrintWindow` with `PW_RENDERFULLCONTENT` renders
the XAML layers but not Win2D's DirectX swap chain, so the 9:16 frame comes out **blank white**
while every control around it is perfect. That looks exactly like a renderer that drew nothing.
Use `PrintWindow` for Help and Settings, which hold no Win2D; use `CopyFromScreen` for anything
showing a preview, and confirm a blank frame against a second method before believing it.

**`SetForegroundWindow` fails silently from a background process.** Windows blocks foreground
steals, the call returns false, and `CopyFromScreen` then captures whatever *is* on top — which
in practice is the editor, at the app's coordinates, looking like the app rendered someone
else's UI. `CopyFromScreen` is only trustworthy once the window is verified frontmost.

**A 160×28 window rect means the window is minimized, not that the handle is wrong.** That is the
rect Windows reports for a minimized window, and `GetWindowRect` returns it without complaint, so
a capture silently produces a 160×28 image of the desktop. UIA is blunter about it: the element's
`BoundingRectangle` comes back infinite, which fails as "Value was either too large or too small
for an Int32".

This was first misdiagnosed here as `MainWindowHandle` naming a transient helper window, and the
wrong fix — polling for a handle whose rect looks big enough — happens to work only because a
minimized window fails that test too. Restore first, then check:

```powershell
if ([W]::IsIconic($h)) { [W]::ShowWindow($h, 9) }   # SW_RESTORE
$r = ...; if (($r.Rt - $r.L) -lt 600) { throw "refusing to capture a ${w}-wide window" }
```

Have the capture helper **refuse** a rect that small rather than saving it. A 160×28 PNG is a
result that looks like a rendering failure, and it was read as one twice.

**Do it in one command.** There is no notification-area icon, so closing the window ends the
app. A handle captured in one shell call is routinely dead by the next, surfacing as
`ElementNotAvailableException` from `AutomationElement.FromHandle`. Launch, navigate and capture
in a single script.

## A PowerShell helper that returns a string returns it as output

A bare string inside a function joins the **return value**, so this is always truthy:

```powershell
function Click($n) { if (-not $el) { "  NOT FOUND"; return $false } }
if (Click "Stock Volume") { Shot }      # runs even on NOT FOUND
```

`@("  NOT FOUND", $false)` is a two-element array, and an array is truthy. That is how three
navigation clicks silently did nothing while the script reported success and captured the same
page three times. Use `Write-Host` for diagnostics inside a function, and return one value.

It is the same failure the rest of this file is about: a check that cannot fail is not a check.
Drive navigation by **index** rather than by name, too — the label depends on which of fourteen
languages the app started in, and the console mangles CJK to `?` so a name comparison looks
broken when it is only unprintable.

## Land a risky UI change as its own commit

Anything that restructures a page's layout or the preview cannot be verified here beyond
compiling and checking resources — it has to be looked at. Commit it alone, so the look can be
answered with a revert rather than an unpicking.
